str_detect() with filter()

Which colleges in the dataset have “Polytechnic” in their name?


colleges |&gt; 
  filter(str_detect(INSTNM, pattern = "Polytechnic"))



How many colleges in the dataset have “Polytechnic” in their name?


colleges |&gt; 
  summarize(n = sum(
    str_detect(INSTNM, pattern = "Polytechnic")
    )
    )

str_remove()

Remove the first matched pattern in each string.

colleges |&gt; 
  mutate(INSTNM = str_remove(INSTNM, 
                             pattern = "(College|University)"
                             )
         )










Related Functions


This is a special case of str_replace(x, pattern, replacement = "").
str_remove_all() removes all matched patterns in each string.

Change the Length

shorten or lengthen a string to a specified length


str_sub()str_pad()



Extract values of a string based on a starting and ending location.


colleges |&gt; 
  mutate(short_name = str_sub(INSTNM, 
                                  start = 1,
                                  end = 8)
         )




Make every string have a fixed length (width)


colleges |&gt; 
  mutate(long_name = str_pad(INSTNM, 
                             width = 20, 
                             pad = "_", 
                             side = "both")
         )

str_trim()

removes whitespace from start and end of string


colleges |&gt; 
  mutate(
    INSTNM = str_trim(INSTNM, side = "both")
         )

pull()
What is the mean potassium for cold cereals? 🧩


You can’t use the $ operator in a pipeline
pull() to the rescue!
pull() extracts a data frame column as a vector




cereal |&gt; 
  filter(type == "cold") |&gt; 
  pull(potass) |&gt; 
  mean(na.rm = T)

[1] 97.21622

Using kable() for formatting in labs

When printing rows of a data frame or tibble
need to load the knitr package at the beginning of your file
kable() outputs a markdown version of your data
should only be used to nicely format data you are printing



e.g.

cereal |&gt; 
  mutate(ratio = sugars / potass) |&gt; 
  slice_head(n = 3) |&gt; 
  kable()


NOT

cereal_clean &lt;- cereal |&gt; 
  mutate(ratio = sugars / potass) |&gt; 
  slice_head(n = 3) |&gt; 
  kable()

County Revenue Data
Data collected from the California State Controller. San Franscisco County is excluded.

2003 - 2016 data

dat_03_16 &lt;- read_excel("CountiesRawData_20180613.xlsx",
                        sheet = "CO_REV_PROP_OTHR_TAXES") |&gt; 
  clean_names() |&gt; 
  select(entity_name, year = fiscal_year,
         total_property_taxes, sales_and_use_taxes)



2017 data

dat_17 &lt;- read_excel("CountiesRawData_20181228.xlsx",
                        sheet = "CO_REV_PROP_OTHR_TAX",
                     na = c("", "NULL")) |&gt; 
  clean_names() |&gt; 
  select(entity_name, year = fiscal_year,
         sales_and_use_taxes = sales_and_use_taxes_total_governmental_funds_taxes_other,
         total_property_taxes = total_property_taxes_total_governmental_funds_property_taxes) 



2018 data

dat_18 &lt;- read_excel("CountiesRawData_FY2018-2020_20210926_V4.xlsx",
                        sheet = "CO_REV_PROP_OTHR_TAXES") |&gt; 
  clean_names() |&gt; 
  select(entity_name, year = fiscal_year,
         sales_and_use_taxes = sales_and_use_taxes_total_governmental_funds_taxes_other,
         total_property_taxes = total_property_taxes_total_governmental_funds)



Append and clean

tax_dat &lt;- dat_03_16 |&gt; 
  rows_append(dat_17) |&gt; 
  rows_append(dat_18) |&gt; 
  arrange(entity_name, year) |&gt; 
  filter(year %in% 2005:2018) |&gt; 
  mutate(entity_name = str_c(entity_name, " County"))

Check that county names align with other lab data

counties &lt;- read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/master/data/2023/2023-05-09/counties.csv')


tax_counties &lt;- tax_dat |&gt; 
  group_by(entity_name) |&gt; 
  summarize(mean_tax = mean(total_property_taxes))


ca_counties &lt;- counties |&gt; 
  filter(state_abbreviation == "CA") |&gt; 
  left_join(tax_counties, by = join_by(county_name == entity_name))

Quick check
How many rows and columns will each of these data frames have?

cereal |&gt; 
  group_by(manuf) |&gt; 
  mutate(fiber_carb_rat = mean(fiber / carbo))



cereal |&gt; 
  group_by(manuf) |&gt; 
  summarize(fiber_carb_rat = mean(fiber / carbo))








Tip


The cereal data starts with 77 rows and 16 columns. There are 7 manufacturers in the data.

Save your changes!
If you need to use your manipulated data in another part of your code, make sure you assign your new dataset to a variable!

cereal_summary &lt;- cereal |&gt; 
  filter(type == "cold") |&gt; 
  mutate(potass_per_cup = potas / cups) |&gt; 
  group_by(manuf) |&gt; 
  summarise(mean_potass_per_cup = mean(potass_per_cup))

If you don’t need that table again… no need to save a new object
